WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.120
Film Courage: What is the most nervous you’ve
been as a performer?

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.730
Jonathan Mangum, Actor/Comedian/Announcer
for Let’s Make a Deal: The most nervous?

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:11.240
Ahhh…I went and screen tested for Saturday
Night Live and there were five of us. They

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.870
fly you to New York, get up on stage and I
knew they were going to tape me but I didn’t

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.769
realize they were going to tape me with the
actual cameras that they…the big cameras.

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:25.590
I mean it wasn’t like a little video camera
thing. The camera dude was there with the

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.830
camera. You are on the monologue stage and
at the table was Tina Fey and Lorne Michaels

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:38.460
and two or three other people and that was
SO nerve-racking because there are only 5

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.800
people watching you. Even if what you’re
doing is hilarious and they’re laughing,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.890
it’s not going to sound like a wall of laughter.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.989
And that year it was Fred Armisen, Will Forte,
my good friend Jeff Davis and another guy

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:56.730
I kind of knew. All five of us were going
up the elevator going “Hey man good luck

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:05.260
to you!” “Good luck to you!” And we
were all in the same boat together. So we

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:12.620
went in and did our audition which I thought
it did pretty well and we are walking out

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:19.220
and all of us are getting into the elevator
and were were “Oh, you guys want to go get

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.110
a drink? That was crazy, that was scary. You
guys want to go get a drink?” And right

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.780
as the doors are closing someone’s hand
goes “Fred, can you come with us?” And

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.581
we were like “Aaarrrghh! Couldn’t you
have waited for tomorrow to tell him he got

00:01:31.581 --> 00:01:36.920
it so we could all bond and have a good time?

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.530
That was super scary but also I think I mentioned
earlier it’s not about who is best. It’s

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:47.750
about what they need at the time. Did they
need a six foot two white guy at the time?

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:54.680
It’s all about fits into the cast at that
moment so I feel better about that now but

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.250
afterwards I was like “Oh I must have been
terrible.” But I wasn’t.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:04.271
Film Courage: Was that another lesson on not
getting your hopes up to much? When you get

00:02:04.271 --> 00:02:06.490
an opportunity like that I’m sure you’re
super excited?

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:11.500
Jonathan: The problem with screen-testing
for anything in this town and getting your

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:18.660
hopes up is you have to sign the contract
before you test. So you have to sign a piece

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.480
of paper that shows you exactly how much money
you’re going to make every month and how

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:29.920
many years. You sign the deal before you test.
Everyone who tests for every sitcom and every

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.700
sitcom, every show on TV there are going to
be three or four people that go in for the

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.450
network test and before they do they all have
to…so you’re looking at the money like

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:43.200
“Oh my gosh, I could have all of this money?”
You have to sign it ten times and initial

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.340
on 15 pages and turn it in and then you audition.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:50.870
So yeah…you shouldn’t get your hopes up.
You should just do your best but at the time

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.721
it’s like “Oh my gosh.” You’ve seen
all the numbers of that piece of paper. Just

00:02:54.721 --> 00:02:57.630
in the back of your mind you’re like “Oh,
I could really use all that money.”

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.310
Film Courage: Well that is where your psychology
degree came in handy.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.670
Jonathan: Right, I should have listened. If
I’d gotten a B I probably would have been

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:05.670
happier.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.910
Film Courage: It would be very difficult to
see that and go in and audition.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:16.330
Jonathan: It’s tough, yes. Network testing
is the scariest thing. And of all of the ones

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.260
I’ve tested for SNL was the most scariest.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.620
Film Courage: Yes, I think Marc Maron spoke
about how he auditioned and waiting outside

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.190
and “Yeah, thanks kid.”

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:30.690
Jonathan: Marc was bitter about it for years
and then if you listened he had Lorne on his

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:37.880
podcast [WTF with Marc Maron] and they had
a nice…what’s the word? Reconciliation.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.630
Film Courage: The nice thing is he’s upfront
about being bitter. I know some people criticize

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:45.290
him for that. I think it’s great because
he’s pretty much vocalizing what most people

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:51.700
think anyway. But I know he’s been criticized
for that. But it’s a great story.

